@Strings
.
=STRINGS
.
.DIM name$ * size makes a string of up to size characters, 1 to 255. The form
.name AS STRING * size does the same. A string must have a size.
.
:DIM L$ * 80, N AS STRING * 20
:N = "ADA"
:L$ = "HELLO, " + N
:PRINT L$; LEN(L$)
:IF L$ <> "" THEN PRINT MID$(L$, 1, 5)
.
.  - A main program string is empty when the program starts. A string DIMed
.    in a SUB is set empty each time its DIM line runs.
.  - An assignment cuts the text at the string's size.
.  - + joins two strings. A joined string is cut at 255 characters.
.  - The six compares work on strings. The first character that differs
.    decides, by its code. When one string starts with the other, the longer
.    one is greater.
.  - A string and a number in one operator stop the compile with TYPE
.    MISMATCH.
.  - A string holds any byte, 0 included.
.  - @L$ is the address of the string's block: the size byte, the length
.    byte, then the characters.
.  - Strings sit in memory after the program, as arrays do, and take no space
.    in the PRG file.
.
.The functions:
.
.  - LEN(s) is the length, a UBYTE.
.  - ASC(s) is the code of the first character, or 0 when s is empty.
.  - CHR$(n) is the one character with code n.
.  - STR$(n) is n in decimal, with a space or a minus sign in front: STR$(5)
.    is " 5" and STR$(-3) is "-3".
.  - VAL(s) is the number s starts with. Spaces and a + before it are
.    skipped. A - right before the digits gives the bits of the negative
.    number: PRINT VAL("-42") prints 65494, and X = VAL("-42") with X AS INT
.    gives -42. VAL("12AB") is 12, and VAL("ABC") is 0. VAL gives a 16-bit
.    value, also when it is stored in a LONG.
.  - LVAL(s) is the number s starts with, read in 32 bits, as a ULONG. Stored
.    in a LONG it is the signed value: L = LVAL("-1234567") with L AS LONG
.    gives -1234567, and PRINT LVAL("3000000000") prints 3000000000. LVAL is
.    a reserved word.
.  - MID$(s, start, count) is count characters of s from position start. The
.    first position is 1, and 0 is the same as 1. Without count, it is the
.    rest of s. A start past the end gives "".
.  - STR.name is the same function as name: STR.MID$ is MID$. STRINGS.UXI
.    lists them in NAMESPACE STR.
.  - INSTR(s, find, start) is the position of the first find in s at or after
.    position start, a UBYTE. It is 0 when there is none, or when find is
.    empty. Without start, the search starts at 1, and 0 is the same as 1.
.
.LEN and ASC of a literal, and CHR$ and STR$ of a constant, are worked out by
.the compiler.
.
.A SUB takes a STRING parameter with no size: SUB SHOW(T$). The SUB works on
.the caller's string, so an assignment to T$ changes it. A literal argument
.is copied first, so the SUB cannot change the literal.
.
.Not yet: string arrays, a start value.
.
.See Types, Subroutines, Including files.
.
